N5008 [dcl.type.auto.deduct] Example 3 and Example 2, [dcl.spec.auto.general]
Examples 1, 3 and 4: placeholder type deduction for variables (`auto`,
`const auto *`, `auto &`, `const auto &`, `auto &&` bound to an lvalue and to
an xvalue, `decltype(auto)` of an id-expression, a parenthesized one, an
xvalue and a member access, a parenthesized initializer `auto x(i)`), for
return types (plain, ptr-operator forms, `decltype(auto)` forms, void from no
return statement, a recursive call after the first return statement, two
returns deducing the same type, array decay) and for lambdas (deduced value,
void, `decltype(auto)`).  One deduction routine now serves all of them;
before, three return-type paths and the variable path each had their own
rules and disagreed (`decltype(auto) f() { return i; }` gave `int &`,
`auto x2a(i)` and `auto &&r = f()` with f returning T&& were rejected).
Non-vacuous: the last assertion states a wrong value and must FAIL.
